<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03JY">Construction <span data-tag="03JY">8.1.2.7</span>.</a> Let $f: K \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a morphism of simplicial sets, let $\sigma $ be an $n$-simplex of $K^{\operatorname{op}}$, and let $\tau $ be an $n$-simplex of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{f/}$. Then the composition </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  ( \Delta ^{n} )^{\operatorname{op}} \star \Delta ^{n} \xrightarrow { \sigma ^{\operatorname{op}} \star \tau } = K \star \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{f/} \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}} \]
</div>
<p> can be identified with an $n$-simplex $T(\sigma ,\tau )$ of $\operatorname{Tw}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$. The construction $(\sigma , \tau ) \mapsto T(\sigma ,\tau )$ determines a morphism of simplicial sets $K^{\operatorname{op}} \times \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{f/} \rightarrow \operatorname{Tw}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$, which we can identify with a map $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{f/} \rightarrow \operatorname{Fun}_{ / \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\operatorname{op}} }( K^{\operatorname{op}}, \operatorname{Tw}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) )$ which we refer to as the <em>coslice inclusion</em>. We will be particularly interested in the special case where $K = \Delta ^0$, so that $f$ can be identified with a vertex $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. In this case, we can identify the coslice inclusion with a morphism of simplicial sets $\iota _ X: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{X/} \rightarrow \{ X\}  \times _{ \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\operatorname{op}} } \operatorname{Tw}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$, which we will denote by $\iota _{X}$. </p>
</article>
